 "analysis": {
  "clock": "SM clock 2.879 GHz (clock64 vs %globaltimer); 1 ns = 2.88 cycles",
  "L2_round_trip": "single thread, 64 lines 64 KiB apart: ld.cg 338-392 cycles (median 354), atomicAdd with return 363-421 (median 382), ld.acquire 345-399. No near/far split larger than ~15% was seen from this SM; the chase-latency spread in gather_designs (354-870) came from the chase footprint, not from a remote partition.",
  "Q1_why_L2_synchronisation_is_slow": [
   "It is latency, not serialisation. Counter barrier vs participants: N=1 473 ns, N=188 666 ns; fit over N=8..188 gives 649 ns + 0.090 ns per arrival (0.26 cycles). Same-word atomics sustain one per 0.41 ns (1.2 cycles), so even 188 simultaneous arrivals serialise for <= 77 ns.",
   "The fence is the single largest term. membar.gl alone = 458 cycles, after a store = 713, st.release = 679, fence.acq_rel = 480: a GPU-scope fence waits for the SM's outstanding writes to be acknowledged by L2, i.e. at least one L2 round trip (~355-420 cycles) plus drain.",
   "One-way handoff: bare st.volatile -> ld.volatile poll = 376-427 cycles (134-152 ns), about one L2 round trip; with release/acquire or volatile+fence = 950-1097 cycles (333-384 ns). The ~600-cycle difference is the fence; the flag line location changes it by <10%.",
   "Polling quantisation: a poll loop of period D adds ~D/2 (relaxed poll with 0/200/500/1000/2000/4000-cycle delay: [434, 542, 684, 936, 1439, 2429] cycles one-way). The counter barrier polls with an atomic round trip (~383 cycles), so detection adds ~190 cycles on average; the counter was polled 3.44 times per boundary.",
   "Reading a line another SM just wrote costs 407 cycles vs 396 for an untouched line: no extra coherence trip (L2 is the point of coherence, L1 is bypassed by .cg/.volatile).",
   "Budget of the 659 ns (1,900-cycle) barrier: arrive-side fence ~460 + atomic arrive landing ~190 + poll round trip ~383 plus half a poll period ~190 + consumer-side fence ~460 = ~1,680 cycles, plus the spread of 188 arrival times (N=1 -> N>=8 adds 176 ns). That is ~5 L2 round trips, of which two are fences.",
   "Moving the data adds more L2 time: 188 SMs reading the SAME 128 B line with 8 warps each get one line-request per 0.79 ns per line (3411 cycles per dependent load vs 365 for private lines, 862 with 8 replicas). A 16 KiB vector is 128 lines x 188 readers = 24,064 line requests per boundary; that queueing is why one copy costs 614 ns and 8 replicas 377 ns (gather_designs 1b vs 1c)."
  ],
  "Q2_why_the_megatron_pair_is_slower": [
   "An all-reduce IS a reduce-scatter plus an all-gather: two all-SM dependencies. The explicit RS+AG measured barrier 1 = 1345 ns and barrier 2 = 796 ns plus data phases (write partial 577, read 188 slices + sum 634, all-gather read 1184), total 4612 ns. The pair therefore has the same dependency count as the two gathers it replaces, not one.",
   "It also moves 188x the data: every SM publishes a full 4096-float partial (188 x 16 KiB = 3.08 MB written per all-reduce) where a gather publishes 188 x ~88 B = 16.4 KB. Readers still pull ~16 KiB each in both cases.",
   "With red.add the 3.08 MB lands on the SAME 128 lines: the red phase alone takes 3155 ns into a shared target vs 909 ns into private targets (0.98 vs 3.39 TB/s). Each line takes 188 SMs x 8 red.v4 = 1,504 serialised 16 B updates, ~6 cycles each. Phases of the red design (fastest trial): issue 1020, fence waiting for own reds 1671, arrive + wait for all 1239, readback 964 ns.",
   "Net: in this binary one counter gather costs 2210 ns and the all-reduce 4612 (RS+AG) / 4930 ns (red) = 2.1-2.2x; in gather_designs 5a/1b = 3,645/1,638 = 2.2x. All-reduce = two dependencies + 188x the data, so it can at best tie two gathers."
  ],
  "Q3_why_LL_flags_and_clusters_lost": [
   "LL (flag in data): it removes the fence (one-way 967 -> ~400 cycles) but turns polling into data reads. Counted per SM per boundary: LL8 1.62 poll rounds per thread on average (max 4), 2585 16-B loads = 41.4 KB read for 16 KB useful (2.5x); 188 SMs -> 7.8 MB of L2 reads per boundary vs 3.1 MB for the counter design, all on the same 256 lines of one copy, so it queues on the hot slices. LL16 with 8 replicas spreads the lines and wins (1,151 ns in gather_designs), still reading 38.4 KB per SM (1.91 rounds).",
   "Per-producer flags keep both fences (producer st+membar 713 cycles, consumer fence.acq_rel 480) and add hot polled lines: the 188 flags pack into 6 lines, each polled by 188 SMs x 2.00 rounds (283 flag loads per SM per boundary) while receiving 32 writes. Hammering a flag line with 64-186 pollers raises the one-way latency from ~400 to [568, 600] cycles; hammering a different line changes nothing ([401, 400]). The data read still starts only after the last flag.",
   "Sharding the counter lowers arrival contention that was never the bottleneck and multiplies polling: slope 1.07 ns/arrival with 8 shards and 2.88 with 32 (1 shard with ld.acquire: 0.71; atomic poll: 0.09), because every SM polls K lines.",
   "Clusters: cluster.sync is cheap (149 ns = 430 cycles) and a remote ld.shared::cluster is fast in latency (220 cycles vs 34 local smem, 364 L2), but DSMEM throughput is ~1.32 B/cycle per SM whether 1 or 8 warps issue (16 KiB in 4324 ns) vs 6.89 B/cycle from L2 in the same launch and 36.2 from local smem. Moving the vector across the cluster costs ~4.3 us, and the hierarchical barrier still pays the same L2 fence + round trips between clusters on top of cluster.sync."
  ],
  "caveats": [
   "Absolute gather/all-reduce times in this binary run ~35% above gather_designs.cu (counter gather 2,210 vs 1,638 ns; red all-reduce 4,930 vs 3,645) with the same algorithms; gather_designs 1b itself varied 1.64-1.94 us between processes. The likely cause is where the hot 16 KiB vectors land among L2 slices, which changes the hot-spot queueing. Ratios within one binary are what the causal claims use.",
   "E timings include the counting instrumentation; its counts are the point.",
   "The L2 read in G (825 ns per 16 KiB with 8 warps under a cluster launch) is slower than gather_designs private copies (401 ns); DSMEM is compared against the L2 figure from the same launch."
  ]
 },
